<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Ukhaydirid Emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">866 CE – 1050 CE (approx.) · capital al-Khidrima</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Alid dynasty that ruled the oases of al-Yamama in central Arabia for nearly two centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 866 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-yusuf-al-ukhaydir">Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Ukhaydir</a>, a descendant of al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali">Hasan ibn Ali</a>, fled the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Hejaz</a> after a failed revolt and took control of al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamama">Yamama</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a>. He founded a dynasty with its seat at al-Khidrima, in the region of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kharj">al-Kharj</a> south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Ukhaydirids governed farming oases and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribes around them. Later sources describe them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaydism">Zaydi Shi'is</a>. Their land lay on pilgrimage and trade routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-bahrain">Bahrain</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Hejaz</a>, and the Persian traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-khusraw">Nasir Khusraw</a>, who passed in the 1050s, mentions the fortified seat of its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alids">Alid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The rise of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qarmatians-of-bahrain">Qarmatians of Bahrain</a> to the east reduced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> to a client. It faded in the mid-11th century, with little recorded about its end, and al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamama">Yamama</a> fell under local tribal chiefs.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 866 CE – 1050 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Arabia and the Gulf</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:76.94%;width:3.66%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>866 CE – 1050 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>al-Khidrima</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Muhammad ibn Yusuf al-Ukhaydir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Zaydi Shi'a Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Saudi Arabia</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ukhaydirids, Banu Ukhaydir</dd></dl></aside></div>

